              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Nitrogen is used in plastic welding primarily because it acts as a **shielding gas** to prevent **oxidation**. \n\nWhen plastic is heated to its melting point in order to weld it, the process is highly sensitive to the surrounding environment. Here is a detailed breakdown of why nitrogen is the industry standard for creating high-quality plastic welds:\n\n### 1. It Prevents Oxidation (Burning)\nNormal air is composed of approximately 21% oxygen and 78% nitrogen. If you use a standard hot-air welder (which uses compressed ambient air), the combination of extreme heat and oxygen causes the melted plastic to oxidize. \n* **With Hot Air:** The plastic literally begins to burn, char, and degrade. You will often see smoke, discoloration, soot, and even sparks in the weld zone.\n* **With Nitrogen:** Nitrogen is chemically non-reactive (inert) at plastic welding temperatures. By forcing pure nitrogen through the welder, it pushes all the oxygen out of the weld zone. The plastic melts cleanly without burning.\n\n### 2. It Produces Much Stronger Welds\nWhen plastic oxidizes, its molecular chains break down, introducing impurities, char, and microscopic voids into the joint. This makes the resulting weld brittle and highly prone to failure. \nBy shielding the weld with nitrogen, the plastic molecules of both the base material and the welding rod can fuse together flawlessly at the molecular level. This results in a weld that is structurally sound and often just as strong as the original, undamaged plastic.\n\n### 3. Cleaner and Safer Work Environment\nBecause the plastic is melting rather than combusting under a nitrogen shield, the process is much cleaner:\n* There is **virtually no smoke or soot**, meaning a cleaner finish that requires less sanding or prep work before painting.\n* It **minimizes toxic fumes**, making the environment significantly safer and more pleasant for the technician.\n\n### 4. Critical for Specific Plastics (like PP and PE)\nMany modern plastics\u2014especially those used in the automotive industry (like Polypropylene/PP and Polyethylene/PE used in car bumpers)\u2014are incredibly sensitive to thermal oxidation. Attempting to weld PP or PE with standard hot air almost always results in a failed, brittle weld. Nitrogen is mandatory for these materials to achieve a lasting repair.",
